<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Bill of Rights Act 1990 is a statute that sets out civil and political rights, such as freedom of expression, religion and movement, the right to vote and rights in criminal procedure. It is not entrenched and does not allow courts to strike down legislation, but laws are to be interpreted consistently with it where possible. It forms part of New Zealand's unwritten constitution.</p></div>
